Extracted PICO elements from 4 randomized trials of adjuvant progestagens in endometrial cancer. Large multicentre 
trials showed no survival/recurrence benefit and one reported higher non-cancer mortality with progestagens, while a 
single-institution RCT reported improved overall survival (log-rank P<0.001). Safety details largely not reported.

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
- Study 1 (multicentre, Stage I, n=856 ITT): MPA 100 mg BID ×12 months vs no MPA across risk strata (with/without 
radiotherapy). At 84 months, no significant difference in relapse-free survival; cure rate not improved; adverse events 
not reported.
- Study 5 (Stage I–II, n=1148): Progestagen vs observation; median follow-up 72 months (42–132). Crude survival and 
relapse similar; higher non-cancer mortality in progestagen group (P=0.04). Among cancer-specific deaths, median 
survival longer with progestagen (30 vs 22 months; P=0.03). High-risk subgroup (n=461) showed a tendency toward better 
DFS and fewer cancer deaths but unchanged crude survival.
- Study 9 (Stage IA/IB, n=35): 6‑methyl‑17α‑hydroxyprogesterone weekly ×14 starting within 24h post-op vs placebo. At 5 
years, cancer deaths 2/18 vs 1/17; no improvement in survival or recurrence; adverse events not reported.
- Study 10 (single institution, n=205): Progestagen vs observation; 5-year follow-up. Survival significantly longer with
progestagens (log-rank P<0.001); agent/dose not specified; adverse events not reported.

